int('---')\n\ngrab([4], ['categor', 'three', 'reason', 'act', 'interact'])\ngrab([5], ['Google Scholar', 'snowball', 'search'])\ngrab([10], ['Self Consistency', 'majority'])\ngrab([23], ['Toolformer', 'calendar', 'ToolBench'])", "observations": "Execution logs:\n[p4] control, and about the capability to act on one’s goals or will [Epstein and Axtell, 1996, Gilbert, 2019, Barker\n[p4] communicate, and act upon those changes [Wooldridge, 1999], see also Figure 2. Agents have been studied for\n[p4] a long time, and occur in many fields of AI. From the definition of agents interacting with the environment,\n[p4] different approaches focus on specific aspects of agents and agent behavior. In symbolic reasoning [Harman,\n[p4] by rational agents. The field of multi-agent systems studies intelligent systems that emerge from the interaction\n[p4] field of reinforcement learning studies how an agent can learn from interacting with an environment [Sutton\n[p4] and Barto, 2018]. In this context, agents are systems that would adapt their policy if their actions influenced\n[p4] the world in a different way [Kenton et al., 2023]. In autonomous systems and robotics, agents act in order to\n[p4] Agents that receive input in natural language from their environment, reason to make decisions,\n[p4] and take autonomous actions in affecting their environment, to achieve specific goals.\n[p4] We stress that: agents may receive input and reason in natural or formal language; agents may plan to break\n[p4] down complex goals into smaller steps; agents may reflect on their own actions; agents may use tools to retrieve\n[p4] new information or to effect their actions; agent may build an internal model of the world; agents may have an\n[p4] interact in a society with humans and other agents; agents may create their own training data.\n[p4] The categories reasoning–acting–interacting build upon each other: the technology that has been developed\n[p4] by the reasoning approaches (category 1) is used for increasingly intelligent acting by assistants. The interactive\n[p4] abilities of the assistants (category 2) enable social simulation experiments. The outcome of assistant actions\n[p4] (category 2) and of these social experiments (category 3) can be used for data augmentation (category 1), to\n[p4] finetune LLMs (which can improve the accuracy of reasoning LLMs, etc.). This virtuous circle is depicted in\n[p4] Figure 1, and attracts interest from LLM researchers to agentic LLM methods [Sutskever, 2024, Guo et al., 2025, Du\n---\n[p5] research tradition across symbolic AI, robotics/autonomous systems, and connectionism/multi-agent modeling,\n[p5] respectively. Agentic LLMs are thus both a recent development and build on decades of research. This is reflected\n[p5] hope to provide clarity about the main approaches, to ease the entry of new researchers into the field. The\n[p5] papers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing\n[p5] that are under submission at the time of inclusion. Here we filter for reputable academic and industrial research\n[p5] Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n---\n[p10] verification method is needed to prevent error-accumulation. A popular approach is Self Consistency [Wang\n[p10] et al., 2022]. Self Consistency is an ensemble approach that samples diverse reasoning paths, evaluates them,\n[p10] and selects the most consistent answer using majority voting. It improves the performance of Chain of Thought\n[p10] Chain of Thought and Self Consistency are being used to train most modern reasoning LLMs, including OpenAI\n---\n[p23] themselves to use tools [Schick et al., 2023]. The Toolformer model is trained to decide which APIs to call, when\n[p23] calendar. Further works extend this to a larger range of tools. ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs\n[p23] from tool documentations, building on ToolBench. ToolAlpaca is a benchmark with over 3938 instances from 400\n---\nLast output from code snippet:\n['Agentic LLM     •   1:23', 'Fig. 13. Multi LLM agent framework with a planner, caller, summarizer [Shen et al., 2024]', 'vision language model, they achieve target object grounding, achieving improved performance on an L-ZSON', 'benchmark.', '3.2.2 Action Tools. As we have seen in Section 2.3, LLM results may be augmented with results that are retrieved', 'from external sources, such as search engines. The ability to call search engines can be generalized to calling', 'other tools. When the application programming interface (API) of these tools is known, LLMs can be integrated', 'easily with them. To an LLM, an API is just another language to learn. Agentic LLMs must be trained to decide', 'when and how to utilize external tools, depending on the task [Shen et al., 2024]. Language models can teach', 'themselves to use tools [Schick et al., 2023]. The Toolformer model is trained to decide which APIs to call, when', 'to call them, what arguments to pass, and how to best incorporate the results into future token prediction. A', 'range of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a', 'calendar. Further works extend this to a larger range of tools. ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs', 'from RapidAPI, a large dataset of publicly available REST APIs.4', 'Another framework is EasyTool [Yuan et al., 2024c], which focuses on structured and unified instructions', 'from tool documentations, building on ToolBench. ToolAlpaca is a benchmark with over 3938 instances from 400', 'APIs [Tang et al., 2023]. A tool-based benchmark for question answering is ToolQA [Zhuang et al., 2023]. Gorilla', 'is a finetuned LLaMa-based model for generating API calls [Patil et al., 2023], also introducing the APIBench', 'benchmark. Many tool calling frameworks have been developed.', 'Selecting the right tool and summarizing 